2026-09-13 | Footer redesign: real navigation columns + a cropped giant wordmark
- Replaced the single row of afterthought links with three columns (Identity+CTA,
  Navigate, Connect), each with an .eyebrow label, matching the labelling
  convention already used by SectionWire elsewhere on the page.
- Added a huge, cropped background wordmark of site.name as a closing signature
  (the "trendy giant name in the footer" pattern). Implementation choices:
  * `aria-hidden="true"` on the wrapper: the name is already the page's
    accessible h1, so this is a repeat, not new information.
  * A fixed-height, `overflow-hidden` band (h-[22vw], min 6rem, max 13rem) with
    the text centered and cropped by its own container rather than by the
    browser's edge, so the crop amount is stable across viewport heights.
  * Opacity kept at 7% (`text-text/[0.07]`), theme-driven via the existing
    color token so it inverts correctly in light mode.
  * Never uses the reserved signal color: texture, not action.
- Known, accepted Lighthouse finding: `color-contrast` flags that node (both
  themes, both viewports), because axe evaluates rendered contrast regardless
  of aria-hidden (aria-hidden removes it from the accessibility tree, not from
  the visual contrast check). This is WCAG 1.4.3's own "incidental text"
  exception: text that is purely decorative and conveys no information has no
  contrast requirement. Getting to 3:1 would need ~45% opacity, which reads as
  a second shouted headline, not a background mark, defeating the point.
  Accepted deliberately rather than "fixed" by inflating the opacity, same
  posture as the two pre-existing anti_slop_scan exceptions in this project.
  Measured (dark theme, alpha -> contrast against #080e11): 7%->1.15, 30%->2.48,
  ~45%+ needed to clear 3:1.
- Tap targets: nav/social/back-to-top links use the same -my-3 py-3 hit-area
  pattern established in the mobile pass, so footer matches the rest of the
  page rather than introducing a fourth convention.

2026-09-13 | Footer wordmark: first name only, and a real fix for the crop
Feedback: "Abdullah Saleem" felt like it was "filling it extra", and the
wordmark looked cut off at the bottom.
- Text changed to the first name only (site.name.split(" ")[0]). It is
  aria-hidden and purely decorative, so it never needed to repeat the full
  name the way the real h1 does.
- The real bug behind "cut off at the bottom": the wrapper had a fixed height
  (h-[22vw], capped 6-13rem) shorter than the text's own line box, by design,
  to get a "cropped signature" look. That container is gone. The wrapper is
  now un-sized (height follows content), so the full glyph box always renders,
  top and bottom, at every viewport.
- Font-size changed from three breakpoint values (26/20/15vw) to one
  `clamp(3rem, 18vw, 13rem)`. Reason found while testing the full name at
  1440px before this change: 22vw with no width cap rendered "ABDULLAH SALEEM"
  1617px wide inside a 1440px wrapper, so overflow-hidden was silently
  clipping real letters off both the first and last character, not just
  trimming whitespace. A single clamp scales identically at every breakpoint
  (the vw-to-viewport ratio cancels out) and the 13rem ceiling stops it
  growing without bound on ultrawide monitors.
- Measured after the fix, no clipping and no page-level horizontal scroll at
  375, 1440 or 1920px: text width stays inside the wrapper's box at all three.
  208px is the effective font-size from 1440px up (clamp ceiling), 67.5px at
  375px (18vw, floor not reached).
